<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Auto Backup ruleset for API 28-30 (android:fullBackupContent). API 31+ ignores this file and
   reads res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml instead; minSdk is 28, so both ship and the two must
   stay semantically identical — change one, change the other.

   Policy: back up the settings the user chose, and nothing else.

   This is an allowlist. A single <include> is what makes it one: once any path is included,
   everything not named is out. So there are deliberately NO <exclude> entries here — an <exclude>
   that sits outside every <include> is redundant by construction, and lint's FullBackupContent
   check reports exactly that as an error. What each omission buys is recorded below instead,
   because the reasoning is the part worth keeping; the elements were not.
-->
<full-backup-content>

    <!-- The only state worth restoring: DataStore Preferences — theme, sort/filter, privilege
         mode, freezer mode, biometric lock and friends. The store name "thor_preferences" is
         declared in data/repository/PreferenceRepositoryImpl.kt:28, and DataStore writes it to
         files/datastore/<name>.preferences_pb, so the domain is "file", NOT "sharedpref" — Thor
         persists nothing through SharedPreferences. A path that matches nothing fails exactly as
         silently as the empty template this replaced, so it has to track that filename.

         The two device-specific keys in here re-resolve rather than break on a new device:
         export_dir_uri is revalidated by ExportAppUseCase (falls back to Downloads if the SAF
         grant is gone) and privilege_mode falls through the Root -> Shizuku -> Dhizuku chain. -->
    <include
        domain="file"
        path="datastore/thor_preferences.preferences_pb" />

    <!--
       Left out on purpose, and each for a different reason:

       - files/app_icons/ — one PNG per installed app, so it grows with the device's app count.
         Cloud backup has a 25 MB per-app quota and overrunning it fails the ENTIRE backup with no
         user-visible error, so this cache alone could silently cost the user their settings.
         AppIconLoader regenerates it on demand.

       - the Room database ("thor_database", built in di/Modules.kt, plus its -wal and -shm
         sidecars) — derived state. The app metadata cache is rebuilt from PackageManager on
         launch, so a restored copy is stale on arrival, and the freezer watchlist sharing the
         same file would name packages the new device may not even have installed.

       - cacheDir — APK staging, split bundles, analysis scratch and installer icons, running to
         hundreds of MB. The platform skips it anyway.

       If any of these ever becomes worth backing up, splitting the user-curated freezer watchlist
       out of the derived metadata cache is the prerequisite, not widening this file.
    -->

</full-backup-content>
